// FIXED. Was: String.is_letter says "every character is a letter, of any script", but
// it is true for digits of other scripts (U+0661 ARABIC-INDIC DIGIT ONE, Nd)
// and combining marks (U+0301, Mn; U+0903, Mc), and false for letter
// numbers (U+216B ROMAN NUMERAL TWELVE, U+3007, Nl). There is no way to ask
// for a character's Unicode category, so gron carries its own tables
// (unicode.plumb, generated from Go's unicode package).
fn main() {
  for c in ["\u{661}", "\u{301}", "\u{903}", "\u{216b}", "\u{3007}", "\u{1c5}"] {
    print("U+${c.bytes().hex()} is_letter=${c.is_letter()}")
  }
}
